# Keep captures and credentials out of the build context entirely.
#
# Not just a build-speed measure: anything copied into an image is recoverable
# from that image's layers even if a later step deletes it. `traces/raw/` holds
# unscanned model transcripts and `.env` holds a real token, so neither should
# ever enter the context in the first place.

.env
.env.local
*.token
mcp_server/mcp.json

# Unscanned captures. Promoted traces under traces/<name>/ have passed the
# secret gate and are fine; traces/raw/ has not.
traces/raw/
evidence/raw/

.git/
.venv/
venv/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.coverage
.coverage.*
coverage.xml
htmlcov/
*.egg-info/
dist/
build/
.DS_Store

# Markdown and `docs/` are NOT excluded, though an earlier revision excluded
# both "to protect the layer cache". Two things were wrong with that.
#
# It protected nothing: the only expensive layer is the pip install, which
# runs *before* `COPY . .`, so a docs edit already invalidated nothing but the
# copy itself. And the image's whole purpose is to run the same suite CI runs
# -- a suite that reads markdown. `tests/test_claude_assets.py` resolves every
# path a skill or agent names, and `.claude/**/*.md` survived only because
# `*.md` matches a single path segment and those files are nested. A skill that
# named `RUNBOOK.md` would have passed on a laptop and failed in the container,
# which is the one disagreement this image exists to settle.
#
# `.github/` is likewise kept: it is small, and excluding it would make the
# container's answer differ from CI's for anything that reads a workflow.
